---
import { Image } from 'astro:assets';
import BaseLayout from '~/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import PageHead from '~/components/PageHead.astro';
import { getCurrentWork } from '~/lib/content';
import { externalLinkProps } from '~/lib/links';
import young from '~/assets/images/me-young.jpg';
import cosplay from '~/assets/images/me-necrodancer.jpg';

/**
 * The "Where I've worked" job list this page used to render (title, company,
 * dates, a `summary` paragraph per job) was cut 2026-08-26 (#135, #141): it
 * duplicated the resume, and the per-job `summary` field it existed to render
 * had no other consumer, so it came out of the content model entirely rather
 * than sitting as unread data — same precedent as dropping jobs' `tech` for
 * #39. The career paragraph below is now the only work overview on this page,
 * and links each era to its project page instead.
 */
/**
 * The current work's facts (#207). This page writes its own sentence around
 * them: the paragraph has just named Second Dinner, so it says "the studio"
 * where the homepage names the company, and it ends on a full stop where the
 * homepage's Currently box takes the site's exclamation. The W4 Games
 * announcement is linked from the whole predicate, so the citation no longer
 * depends on the field containing any particular word.
 */
const { since, doing } = await getCurrentWork();

const w4GamesAnnouncement =
  'https://www.w4games.com/blog/w4-games-news-1/second-dinner-studios-becomes-a-strategic-investor-in-w4-games-and-plans-to-build-the-largest-game-in-godot-yet-37';
const instagramUrl = 'https://www.instagram.com/aliwallick/';
const synagogueUrl = 'https://www.universitysynagogue.org/';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About"
  description="How Ali Wallick got into games, and the four studios since: Kaneva, Red 5 Studios, MobilityWare, and Second Dinner."
  image="/og/home.jpg"
>
  <PageHead title="About" />

  {
    /* The headshot that used to sit here moved to the homepage hero (#181),
      where it keeps the promise /og/home.jpg was already making. What
      replaced it illustrates the paragraph beside it instead of merely
      sitting next to it: the opening line is about getting interested in
      games young, and this is that.

      Square, not the circle the headshot had. A `border-radius: 50%` mask
      crops the corners, and the corners are where the box is — which is the
      whole subject. Shares `.aside-figure` with the cosplay shot below
      rather than inventing a third float width.

      Real alt text, unlike the project thumbnails: this is not duplicating
      adjacent link text, so the model's guard applies normally. */
  }
  <figure class="aside-figure">
    <Image
      src={young}
      alt="Ali as a kid, grinning and holding up a big-box PC game, The Ultimate Titanic Collection, in front of a TV cabinet"
      widths={[300, 600]}
      sizes="14rem"
      loading="eager"
    />
    <figcaption class="meta">Gamer from an early age.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Born and raised in Miami, I got interested in games young. By seven, I was calling my uncle for
    advice on <em>Myst</em>. In high school I found the Computational Media program at Georgia Tech:
    equal parts computer science and digital media. I discovered my love for programming there,
    especially front-end work, where I get to combine the logic of programming with the creativity
    of design. It’s also where I met my husband, Robert, a fellow game developer. I graduated in
    2011 and set out to break into the games industry.
  </p>

  <p>
    I’ve worked in games since then, nearly all of it in UI, feature, and client engineering. Four
    years at <a href="/projects/kaneva">Kaneva</a>, where I went from a new engineer to leading UI
    programming. Then a year on Red 5’s <a href="/projects/firefall">Firefall</a>, a much bigger PC
    shooter. After that, three years shipping casino mobile games at MobilityWare, mostly on{' '}
    <a href="/projects/vegas-blvd-slots">Vegas Blvd Slots</a>. In 2019 I joined Second Dinner as its
    11th employee. I spent five years on <a href="/projects/marvel-snap">Marvel Snap</a>, which
    shipped in October 2022. Since {since}, I’ve been on a new team at the studio,{' '}
    <a href={w4GamesAnnouncement} {...externalLinkProps(w4GamesAnnouncement)}>{doing}</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    I still enjoy talking about the work, going back to my first two panels in 2013. These days it’s
    smaller and more local. In 2020 I made a deck for a Girl Scout troop on what game development is
    actually like. In 2023 I spoke to a college class about the job itself, including overcoming
    anxiety and imposter syndrome. If you have a group that would want one,{' '}
    <a href="/contact">get in touch</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Off the Clock</h2>

  {
    /* Replaced the Kerbal Space Program shot (`me2.jpg`) that stood here from
      #141. That one is the costume the paragraph's award sentence names, but
      it is a picture of two helmets and Ali is not visibly in it — which
      stopped mattering when the headshot was on this page and started
      mattering once it moved to the homepage (#181). This one shows her, and
      still shows a costume she made, which is the point of the slot: it is
      the only image on the site of Ali making something with her hands
      outside work.

      The award sentence is untouched and still true. The photo simply shows a
      different costume than the one that won, which the caption says outright
      rather than leaving a reader to assume this is the Kerbal one. 2019 is
      off the file's own timestamp (2019-08-29), which lands inside Dragon Con
      2019's run — it is not inferred from the venue.

      Floated above both paragraphs, not just the one that names costuming:
      with two paragraphs in this section, a float placed between them only
      wraps the second one, leaving the first at full width and the two edges
      misaligned (#141, caught on the branch preview). Floating it first lets
      both paragraphs wrap it consistently. */
  }
  <figure class="aside-figure aside-figure-crop">
    <Image
      src={cosplay}
      alt="Ali at Dragon Con in a Crypt of the NecroDancer cosplay: long yellow wig, blue headband, orange tunic over a gray long-sleeved shirt, leather belt, fingerless gloves, and tall boots"
      widths={[300, 600]}
      sizes="14rem"
      loading="lazy"
    />
    <figcaption class="meta"><em>Crypt of the NecroDancer</em> cosplay.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <p>
    Most of my free time goes to my family, including my young daughter and our pets. We’re settled
    in Southern California these days, and I love it here. The rest goes to strategy and indie video
    games, reading science fiction and fantasy, camping, and traveling when we can. I also cook and
    bake a lot, enough that I post the results on{' '}
    <a href={instagramUrl} {...externalLinkProps(instagramUrl)}>Instagram</a>. I still make time for
    game jams and personal projects on the side.
  </p>

  <p>
    Costuming is one of the hands-on, creative things I gravitate toward away from the computer.
    I’ve been going to Dragon Con for 20-plus years now, including a Best Group Costume win for a{
      ' '
    }
    <em>Kerbal Space Program</em> cosplay. I’ve also spent the last five years on the board of my synagogue,{
      ' '
    }
    <a href={synagogueUrl} {...externalLinkProps(synagogueUrl)}>University Synagogue</a> in Orange County.
  </p>
</BaseLayout>
